# Two files of one module naming each other through imports.
#   zerg build examples/1g/siblings/main.zg
# Expected output:
#   3
#   8
#   8
#
# THE IMPORT ADDS A SPELLING AND TAKES NONE AWAY. Files of one module share a namespace, so
# `count.width(a)` and a bare `width(a)` are the same call — the import binds a name onto a
# file that is already in this unit rather than loading a second copy of it. Writing it out
# is how a reader of `text.zg` alone can tell where `width` comes from.
import "./lib"

fn main() {
	print lib.width(" abc ")
	print lib.both("ab", " cdef ")
	print lib.plain("ab", " cdef ")
}
